# Line endings for files that end up inside a Linux container.
#
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true, so git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout.
# That is right for Windows editors and catastrophic for a shell script: Linux
# reads the shebang line literally, so `#!/bin/sh\r` sends the kernel looking
# for an interpreter named "sh\r", which does not exist. The container then
# fails with "exec format error" or "no such file or directory" -- naming a file
# that is visibly right there. It is the classic Windows-authored-container bug
# and it costs people hours.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for these paths: LF in the repository, LF in the
# working tree, LF in the image.
#
# Scoped to container files rather than set repo-wide (`* text=auto`), which
# would renormalise every existing file and bury the next real diff.

*.sh            text eol=lf
docker/**       text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf
